 "title": "O Come, All Ye Faithful",
 "description": "The UK version of the Christmas processional: W. H. Monk's four parts from Hymns Ancient and Modern, arranged for 2 to 4 singers, with Frederick Oakeley's English words as that book prints them. Verse 1 and the refrain, in G at a steady walk; the refrain builds as the book marks it, quiet, the bass resting for its second line, then everyone together on the last \"O come, let us adore Him.\"",
 "language": "en",
 "difficulty": "beginner",
 "genre": [
  "christmas",
  "carol",
  "hymn"
 ],
 "style": "straight",
 "tempo": 94,
 "key": "G",
 "timeSignature": "4/4",
 "pickupBeats": 1,
 "credits": {
  "words": "Latin hymn \"Adeste Fideles\" (18th century); English words Frederick Oakeley (1841), as printed in Hymns Ancient and Modern",
  "tune": "\"Adeste Fideles\", attributed to John Francis Wade (c. 1743)",
  "arrangement": "W. H. Monk's four parts (Hymns Ancient and Modern, 1861), adapted by Stackapella"
 },
 "licence": {
  "words": "public domain: Frederick Oakeley's translation (Oakeley died 29 January 1880, Julian's Dictionary of Hymnology as quoted at hymnary.org/person/Oakeley_F) of the 18th-century Latin hymn; verse 1 of the four, with its refrain, as the Revised Edition of Hymns Ancient and Modern prints it under the music, \"Him\" and \"Angels\" capitalised and \"Christ\" and \"Lord\" in small capitals there. The words are our US version's word for word; only the capitals and punctuation differ (\"Born, the King of Angels;\" where the US version has \"born the King of angels:\"), and the book's underlay, kept here: \"come\" in \"O come ye to\" over two notes, \"to\" on the tune's high note, \"Beth-\" over two notes and \"le-\" on a quaver, \"Him,\" before \"Christ\" over two notes, \"Christ\" over two notes and \"the\" on a quaver. The 1861 first edition's words page has \"triumphant;\" and \"O come ye, to Bethlehem;\"",
  "tune": "public domain: the 18th-century Latin hymn tune \"Adeste Fideles\", which the 1861 book's Index of Tunes lists as \"Proper Tune\", naming no composer; the Revised Edition heads it \"Adeste Fideles\", \"Irregular\", also naming none",
  "arrangement": "W. H. Monk's four parts for Hymns Ancient and Modern, No. 42 (1861), signed in that book's Index of Tunes (p. xx), \"Harmonized or Arranged by W. H. Monk\" (Monk died 1889; public domain), as the Revised Edition prints them (No. 59), a tone lower, with the lower parts revoiced for 2 to 4 beginners (pitches named here in G, as written). Where Monk's alto sits a fourth or fifth under the tune or doubles it, the alto takes the chord's third so the duet keeps it: B3 on \"O come, all\" (his D4), with the tenor on G3 beside the bass (his B3, now the alto's note); F sharp 4 on \"faith-ful,\" and \"le-hem;\" (his D4), a third above the tune's low D4 on \"ful\" and \"hem\" as in our US version, the bass on D3 under \"faith-ful,\" (his F sharp 3) and the tenor on A3 under \"le-hem;\" (his F sharp 3) so the leading tone isn't doubled; C4 on \"ye\" before \"to Bethlehem\" (his D4), with the tenor on D3 there (his F sharp 3, doubling the leading tone with the bass); F sharp 4 on \"Born,\" (his D4), over D3 in the bass (his F sharp 3); C4 and C sharp 4 on \"us a-\" in the last line (his D4 and his quavers D4, C sharp 4); and G4 then F sharp 4 on \"Christ\" (his D4 held), with the tenor on B3 then D4 (his G3, F sharp 3) and D4 and B3 on \"the\" (his B3 and G3), so no two parts move in octaves. \"Come and behold Him\": Monk puts the tenor and bass in unison on B3 (C sharp 4 as printed), over the bass's beginner range; here the alto sings B4, A4, G4, F sharp 4, G4, a third under the tune (his D4, quavers E4 F sharp 4, G4, G4 F sharp 4, G4), the tenor keeps his notes, and the bass sings G3 on \"Come\" and F sharp 3 on \"and\" (his B3 and A3, the chord on \"and\" D7 over F sharp, his A minor), then his G3, A3, G3. The tenor sings B3 on \"the\" before \"King\" (his G3) so it doesn't leap a fifth and then a third, and G3 on the last line's \"O\" (his D4) so it doesn't leap a minor sixth across the breath; the bass holds D3 through \"dore\" in the last line (his D3, C3). One note a syllable in every lower part (the alto's B3 under \"come\" before \"ye, O come ye\", where Monk's alto rises B3, C sharp 4; one note for each of his quaver pairs), except where the tune itself slurs two notes on a syllable and Monk's chord changes with it: \"come\" in \"O come ye to\" (tenor A3, G3; bass D3, B2), \"Beth-\" (alto D4, C sharp 4; tenor A3, G3), \"Him,\" before \"Christ\" (alto D4, E4; bass B2, C3) and \"Christ\" (alto, tenor); singable.py lists these as melismas, accepted. The refrain: the Revised Edition prints its first line for the tune alone (p) over small notes for the organ, the bass resting, and its second line (cres.) for soprano, alto and tenor with the bass resting; the bass enters on the third (f). Here the alto sings the organ's tenor line in the first (B3, B3, A3, B3, C4, B3: the voice part the 1861 book prints there) and the tenor and bass hold G3 under it with the tenor's words and rhythm, the held bass note the 1861 book prints there (a D3 pedal at the bottom of the tenor's range would hold a six-four under the tonic for seven beats); the bass rests through the second line as both books print, so the trio sings that line as a duet. Breaths written in for beginners: each line's last note gives up half a beat or a beat, and each \"O\" that starts a line (after \"triumphant,\", \"Angels;\" and the first two \"adore Him,\") comes half a beat late, as in our US version. Accepted exceptions (singable.py): the alto over the tune at \"ful\", \"le-\" and \"hem;\" (above), and the melismas. The duet's one bare fourth on a strong beat is Monk's D4 under the tune's G4 on \"Him,\" before \"Christ\", kept because every other alto there moved in octaves with the bass; the duet and trio otherwise lose the chord's third only on weak beats or short notes, and on \"and\" (beat 35, the tune's C5 over the alto's A4). The alto sings Monk's D4 on \"us\" in the refrain's last line. A breath is written at the comma after \"O come ye,\" in the second line (its \"ye,\" half a beat, the next \"O\" half a beat late, as every line's \"O\" here), so no line runs past about seven seconds; after its fourth down from G4 to \"us\" the alto goes on down a step to C sharp 4 before turning back to D4, Monk's own line. The last chord is Monk's, G, B, G, G, without a fifth. The Amen is left out.",
  "recordings": "synthesized guides",
  "checked": null,
  "source": "Hymns Ancient and Modern for use in the Services of the Church, with Accompanying Tunes, compiled and arranged under the musical editorship of William Henry Monk (London: printed and published for the proprietors by William Clowes and Sons, Limited), the Revised Edition (its preface dated 21 January 1875) in a Complete Edition printing with the Supplemental Hymns (their preface dated 3 April 1889, the supplemental tunes revised by Charles Steggall), No. 59, \"Adeste Fideles\", \"Irregular\", \"42 Orig. Ed.\", pp. 74 and 75, crotchet = 92 (the 1889 preface says metronome marks were added throughout), in A major (three sharps), 4/4 with a crotchet pickup, written here a whole tone lower in G (the US version's key), so that Monk's bass, which lies at the top of the bass staff through \"O come, all ye faithful\" and \"Come and behold Him\", stays in the beginner range; tempo 94, as our US version, not the printed 92, so the last line fits one breath; read from the Princeton Theological Seminary (Benson Library) copy at archive.org/details/hymnsanci00chur/page/n109 and n110, and checked against another printing of the Revised Edition without metronome marks, No. 59, p. 42, archive.org/details/hymnsancientmode00monk/page/n57, the same notes, small notes, rests and dynamics where compared (the refrain in full). Compared with the first edition with tunes (London: Novello and Co., 1861), No. 42, in A, read from the University of Toronto copy at archive.org/details/hymnsancientmode00unse/page/n73 (words on n74; Index of Tunes on n25, p. xx): the verse is the same except that on \"faith-\" the 1861 alto has D4 over A3 in the bass (1875: E4 over G sharp 3); on \"come ye to\" the 1861 tenor has B3, C sharp 4, B3, E3 (1875: B3, A3, G sharp 3, E3); and \"Beth-le-hem\" is two minims, G sharp 4 and F sharp 4, then E4 (1875: G sharp 4, a minim, and F sharp 4, a dotted crotchet, on \"Beth\", and E4, a quaver, on \"le\"), the alto holding E4 three beats before D sharp 4 and the bass two minims on B2. The 1861 refrain differs more: all four voices sing its first line in full-size notes, the alto's E4 and the tenor's line (the 1875 organ notes) over a held A3 in the bass; in its second line the alto rises to A4 on \"a-dore\" and the tenor to E4; in its third the bass enters on A2 and rises, B2, C sharp 3, D3 (1875: A3, B3, A3, G sharp 3, F sharp 3), and \"Christ the Lord\" is two minims and a dotted minim with no quaver; the 1861 book has repeat dots before the refrain and no dynamics."
